# How Do I Grow Turnips and Parsnips in My Garden?

## Short Answer

Turnips and parsnips are rewarding cool-season root crops that thrive in loose, fertile soil during spring and fall.
Turnips grow quickly and can be harvested in as few as 30 days for greens or 50 days for roots.
Parsnips require more patience, often needing 100 to 130 days to develop their characteristic sweet flavor.
Both crops benefit from direct sowing since they dislike root disturbance during transplanting.
With proper timing and soil preparation, these underrated vegetables can provide harvests from early summer through winter.

## Detailed Explanation

### Understanding These Cool-Season Root Crops

Turnips (Brassica rapa) and parsnips (Pastinaca sativa) belong to different plant families but share a preference for cool growing conditions.
Turnips are part of the brassica family and grow quickly, producing both edible roots and nutritious greens.
Parsnips are in the Apiaceae family alongside carrots and require a much longer growing season to mature.
Both vegetables develop their best flavor after exposure to light frosts, which converts starches to sugars.
The [[root-crops-growing-guide]] provides additional context on cultivating these and other root vegetables.
Understanding the [[brassica-rapa]] species helps you appreciate how turnips fit into the broader brassica family alongside bok choy and napa cabbage.

### Soil Preparation and Site Selection

Both turnips and parsnips demand deeply loosened, stone-free soil to develop well-formed roots.
Raised beds work exceptionally well for root crops because they provide loose, well-drained growing conditions.
The [[cervantes-raised-beds-mound-planting]] approach improves drainage and soil depth for root development.
Remove any rocks, compacted layers, or debris that might cause forked or stunted roots.
Work in compost or well-rotted manure to a depth of at least 12 inches before planting.
Avoid fresh manure or excessive nitrogen, which promotes lush top growth at the expense of root development.
A soil pH between 6.0 and 7.0 is ideal for both crops, ensuring nutrients remain available to the plants.

### Planting Timing and Techniques

Turnips can be sown in early spring, about two to four weeks before the last frost date.
For a fall harvest, sow turnip seeds in late summer, about six to eight weeks before the first expected frost.
Parsnips require a longer season and should be sown as early as the soil can be worked in spring.
Parsnip seeds are notoriously slow to germinate, often taking two to four weeks to emerge.
Soak parsnip seeds overnight before planting to improve germination rates.
Sow turnip seeds about half an inch deep and thin seedlings to two to four inches apart.
Parsnip seeds should be planted half an inch deep with final spacing of three to six inches between plants.
The [[growing-root-vegetables-guide]] offers detailed sowing depth and spacing recommendations for various root crops.

### Watering and Ongoing Care

Consistent moisture is critical for both turnips and parsnips throughout the growing season.
Dry spells cause turnip roots to become woody and develop a bitter, pungent flavor.
Parsnips need steady watering especially during the first months while their deep taproot establishes.
Apply about one inch of water per week from rainfall or irrigation, increasing during hot periods.
Mulch around plants with straw or shredded leaves to conserve moisture and suppress weeds.
Hand-weed carefully around parsnips since their shallow crown roots are easily damaged by cultivation.
The [[solomon-growing-root-crops-guide]] provides excellent guidance on irrigation strategies for root vegetables.

### Pest and Disease Management

Turnips face many of the same pests that affect other brassicas, including flea beetles, cabbage root maggots, and aphids.
Floating row covers installed at planting time exclude many insect pests without the need for chemical controls.
The [[biofumigation-and-brassica-cover-crops-for-pest-management]] technique uses brassica residues to suppress soil-borne pests and diseases.
Parsnips are relatively pest-free but can suffer from carrot rust flies and parsnip webworms.
Crop rotation helps prevent the buildup of brassica-specific diseases in the soil.
The [[companion-planting]] strategy of interplanting with alliums or aromatic herbs can deter many common pests.
Both crops benefit from good air circulation and avoiding overhead watering to reduce fungal disease pressure.
Regular scouting for pest damage allows early intervention before infestations become severe.

### Harvesting and Storage

Turnips can be harvested at any size but are most tender when roots reach two to three inches in diameter.
Parsnips should be left in the ground until after the first frosts for the sweetest flavor.
In fact, parsnips can be left in the ground all winter in many climates and dug as needed.
Use a digging fork to carefully lift both crops to avoid slicing or damaging the roots.
The [[root-crop-harvest-and-storage]] guide covers best practices for keeping root vegetables fresh for months.
Turnip greens should be harvested when young and tender, ideally before the roots fully mature.
Store harvested roots in a cool, humid environment such as a root cellar for extended preservation.

### What About Growing in Containers?

Turnips adapt well to container growing since they mature quickly and remain relatively compact.
Choose a container at least eight inches deep with good drainage holes for turnip production.
Parsnips are more challenging in containers because of their long taproot, though short-rooted varieties exist.
Use a loose, well-draining potting mix without added fertilizer granules that might cause forked roots.
The [[comparison-cold-frame-gardening-vs-container-gardening-guide]] discusses the tradeoffs between different small-space growing methods.
Container-grown turnips need more frequent watering than those in garden beds since pots dry out quickly.
Grow baby turnip varieties like 'Tokyo Cross' or 'Purple Top Milan' for the best results in confined spaces.
Parsnips grown in containers should be harvested before the ground freezes solid if kept outdoors.

### What About Succession Planting for Continuous Harvests?

Turnips are excellent candidates for succession planting because of their fast growth cycle.
Sow small batches of turnip seeds every two weeks from early spring through fall for a steady supply.
Parsnips are typically planted once since they occupy space for the entire growing season.
The [[coleman-succession-planting]] method demonstrates how to coordinate multiple crops for year-round harvests.
Combine succession-sown turnips with long-season parsnips in the same bed to maximize space efficiency.
The [[coleman-winter-succession-planting-sowing-date-calculations-crop-timing]] system helps you calculate precise sowing dates for fall and winter harvests.

## Key Points

- Turnips (Brassica rapa) mature quickly in 30 to 60 days while parsnips need 100 to 130 days.
- Both crops prefer loose, stone-free soil with a pH between 6.0 and 7.0 for best root development.
- Direct sowing is essential since both crops resent root disturbance during transplanting.
- Consistent moisture prevents turnips from turning woody and bitter during dry periods.
- Frost improves parsnip sweetness by converting starches to sugars in the roots.
- Crop rotation and row covers are effective strategies for managing pests on both crops.
